@ 37.2.4.2 - Proto-Čiwere-Winnebago to Winnebago
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan I”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(2):61 – 66; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan II” International Journal of American Linguistics 16(3):113 – 121; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan III”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(4):168 – 178; and cedh audmanh’s changes above, which assisted me in deciphering the vintage phonetic transcription scheme NB: Does not include developments in unstressed non-nasal vowels. L was apparently either /ɹ/ or /l/; Lʲ (Lʸ in the text) was apparently /j/, or maybe /ʎ/. Also, the changes of /p/ before a consonant are unclear, as described within the text. Wolff says that “Winnebago preserved the intermediate stages of *k reflexes”
	p > p, b, m, w / _C
	V > ∅ / _#
	p > b / V_V
	t > t͡ʃ, d͡ʒ
	L > r
	Lʲ > n / _V:[+nas]
	Lʲ > r / _V:[-nas]
	tʲ > x / _ʔ
	{ pr, {sr,xr}, sn, km } > { pVrV, ʃVrV, sVnV, kVwV }
	mt > r
	tk > t͡ʃk, t͡ʃɡ / #_
	t > ∅ / _k, when medial
	xk > ɡ